#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:1
msgid "Crates & Modules"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:3
msgid "Use workspace dependencies (`workspace-deps`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:5
msgid ""
"Always declare shared dependencies in the workspace `[workspace."
"dependencies]` table and reference them with `.workspace = true` in member "
"crates."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:10
msgid ""
"```toml\n"
"# In the workspace root Cargo.toml\n"
"[workspace.dependencies]\n"
"ostd = { path = \"ostd\", version = \"0.17.0\" }\n"
"bitflags = \"2.6\"\n"
"\n"
"# In a member crate's Cargo.toml\n"
"[dependencies]\n"
"ostd.workspace = true\n"
"bitflags.workspace = true\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:22
msgid ""
"Keep the kernel crate graph layered and acyclic (`layered-kernel-crates`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:24
msgid ""
"For modularity, the Asterinas kernel under `kernel/` is decomposed into an "
"acyclic graph of kernel crates arranged in layers. A kernel crate may depend "
"on crates in the same layer or any lower layer. The layers are, from highest "
"to lowest:"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:30
msgid "The assembler crate (`kernel/src/`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:31
msgid "High-level component crates (`kernel/comps/`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:32
msgid "The `aster-core` crate (`kernel/core/`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:33
msgid "Low-level component crates (`kernel/core/comps/`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:34
msgid "Kernel libraries (`kernel/libs/`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:36
msgid ""
"A new kernel subsystem, driver, or utility type should be added as a "
"separate crate outside `aster-core` by default. Add new code directly to "
"`aster-core` only when necessary."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:40
msgid "All these kernel crates may depend on OSTD."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:42
msgid ""
"When lower-layer code needs behavior implemented above it, define the "
"interface and registration mechanism in the lower layer and let the higher "
"component register its implementation."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:46
msgid "Add module-level documentation for major components (`module-docs`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:48
msgid ""
"A module file that serves as an important kernel component (e.g., subsystem "
"entry point, major data structure, driver) should begin with a `//!` comment "
"explaining:"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:52
msgid "What the module does"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:53
msgid "The key types it exposes"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:54
msgid "How it relates to neighboring modules"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:57
msgid ""
"//! Virtual memory area (VMA) management.\n"
"//!\n"
"//! This module defines [`VmMapping`] and associated types,\n"
"//! which represent contiguous regions of a process's virtual address "
"space.\n"
"//! VMAs are managed by the [`Vmar`] tree in the parent module.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:64
msgid "Default to the narrowest visibility (`narrow-visibility`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:66
msgid ""
"Start private, then widen to `pub(super)`, `pub(crate)`, or `pub` only when "
"an actual external consumer requires it."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:71
msgid "// Good — restricted to the parent module\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:76
msgid "// ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:78
msgid "// Bad — unnecessarily wide\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:83
msgid "The `asterinas` assembler crate does not expose any `pub` Rust items."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:85
msgid ""
"Visibility modifiers encode intent, not just effect (`encode-intent-in-vis`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:87
msgid ""
"A plain `pub` says \"export me as far as possible\": along its defining "
"module path, the item's _effective_ visibility is capped by every enclosing "
"module up to the crate root (and a `pub use` elsewhere can re-export it "
"around that cap entirely). In a large project like Asterinas, a `pub` item "
"may sit deep in the module hierarchy, making its effective visibility hard "
"to see locally and fragile against visibility changes in its ancestors."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:97
msgid ""
"Therefore, a visibility modifier in Asterinas declares the maximum "
"_intended_ exposure of an item, regardless of what its ancestors happen to "
"allow:"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:101
msgid ""
"`pub` means \"exported from this crate\". Reserve it for items with an "
"actual consumer in another crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:103
msgid ""
"`pub(crate)`, `pub(super)`, `pub(in super::super)`, and `pub(in crate::a)` "
"mean \"this item must never escape the named scope\", even when an enclosing "
"private module already restricts it further. If the `path` part of a `pub(in "
"path)` modifier is long, apply [`short-vis-path`](#short-vis-path)."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:109
msgid ""
"One exception is struct fields and union fields: a `pub` field of a "
"visibility-restricted struct or union is acceptable, because the field sits "
"only a few lines from the type's own modifier, so its real visibility "
"remains locally obvious."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:114
msgid ""
"This guideline is partially enforced by the rustc lint [`unreachable_pub`]"
"(https://doc.rust-lang.org/rustc/lints/listing/allowed-by-default."
"html#unreachable-pub), which flags any `pub` item not actually reachable "
"from outside its crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:118
msgid "Restrict subsystem visibility with a short name (`short-vis-path`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:120
msgid ""
"To conform with the [`narrow-visibility`](#narrow-visibility) guideline, a "
"common pattern is to expose Rust items inside a subsystem only to the "
"subsystem itself. But when the subsystem sits deep in the module hierarchy, "
"we have to write long visibility modifiers like `pub(in crate::a::very::"
"deep::subsystem)`, which is tedious for writers and unfriendly for readers."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:127
msgid ""
"Instead, you can use the `short_vis_path` macro to create a short name for "
"the long full path of a subsystem:"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:131
msgid "// In kernel/src/a/very/deep/subsystem/lib.rs\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:132
#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:166
msgid "// Good\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:137
msgid "// Bad: violating the narrow-visibility guideline.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:140
msgid "// Bad: the visibility path is too long to read.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:145
msgid ""
"To avoid overusing this attribute, only follow this guideline when **all** "
"of the following three conditions are met:"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:148
msgid ""
"the submodule depth exceeds 2 levels (i.e., the target path contains at "
"least two `::` separators)"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:152
msgid "// In kernel/src/fs/utils/systree_inode.rs\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:153
msgid ""
"// Good: `fs` is a direct submodule of root, thus it's readable already.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:156
msgid "// Bad: no need to do this.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:162
msgid "`pub(super)` and `pub(self)` are inapplicable"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:165
msgid "// In ostd/src/mm/page_table/mod.rs\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:169
msgid "// Bad\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:175
msgid "and the restricted visibility path is used at least 2 times"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:177
msgid ""
"Refer to [\\#3188](https://github.com/asterinas/asterinas/issues/3188) for "
"the `short-vis-path` design."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:182
msgid "Qualify function calls with the parent module (`qualified-fn-imports`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:184
msgid ""
"When importing a free function or a static/constant from another module, "
"import the **parent module** and access the item through it (`module::"
"function()`, `module::CONSTANT`). Do not import free functions or statics "
"directly by name."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:190
msgid ""
"This convention is recommended by [_The Rust Programming Language_](https://"
"doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch07-04-bringing-paths-into-scope-with-the-use-"
"keyword.html) and followed by the Rust compiler codebase. It serves two "
"purposes:"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:195
msgid ""
"The call site makes it clear that an imported item is being used, not a "
"local one."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:198
msgid "The module name provides context that complements the item name."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:202
msgid "// Good — module-qualified function call\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:206
msgid "// Good — module-qualified static access\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:211
msgid "// Bad — bare function name; unclear origin at call site\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:216
msgid "// Bad — bare static name; could be mistaken for a local constant\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/rust-specific/crates-and-modules.md:223
msgid ""
"This guideline applies to **free functions and statics/constants**. Types, "
"traits, and enum variants should still be imported directly by name, "
"following the standard Rust convention."
msgstr ""
